        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §§IV.A–IV.B",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "André Thess, The Lieb–Yngvason Machine: A didactical tool for the definition of entropy in classical thermodynamics, §§2–3",
              "url": "https://www.dlr.de/de/tt/downloads/dokumente/institut/institutsleitung/Entropie.pdf/@@download/file"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 9
      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-td-contact-product-comparison",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Conditional contact calibrator and product comparison",
        "statement": "For every declared finite family of simple-system domains closed under the thermal joins of [[def-td-thermal-join]], assume the accessibility axioms A1–A6, the simple-system assumptions A7 and S1–S3, the contact assumptions T1–T4, the universal thermal-equilibrium range T5, and equilibrium-join coherence C1, with the exact formulations in [[post-td-accessibility-a1-a6]], [[post-td-simple-system-geometric-axioms]], [[post-td-thermal-join-axioms]], [[post-td-universal-thermal-range]], and [[post-td-equilibrium-join-coherence]]. Then for any two simple types $i,j$, there are $x_0\\prec\\prec x_1$ in $i$ and $y_0\\prec\\prec y_1$ in $j$ such that\n\n$$ (x_0,y_1)\\sim(x_1,y_0). $$\n\nMoreover, for this fixed pair $i,j$, every finite separated product made only from positive scaled copies of $i$ and $j$ satisfies the comparison hypothesis: any two equal-formal-mass states $A,B$ of that product type satisfy $A\\prec B$ or $B\\prec A$. No comparison conclusion for products involving a third primitive type is contained in this pairwise result. These are conditional model deductions. In particular, the conclusion is not claimed from the printed Theorem 4.8 interface with T1–T4 alone; the local mathematical supplier explicitly requires the additional T5 and C1 branch.",
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison",
          "def-td-thermal-join",
          "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6",
          "post-td-equilibrium-join-coherence",
          "post-td-simple-system-geometric-axioms",
          "post-td-thermal-join-axioms",
          "post-td-universal-thermal-range",
          "thm-tdmath-conditional-contact-calibrator"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-thermal-join": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6": "physical-assumption",
          "post-td-equilibrium-join-coherence": "physical-assumption",
          "post-td-simple-system-geometric-axioms": "physical-assumption",
          "post-td-thermal-join-axioms": "physical-assumption",
          "post-td-universal-thermal-range": "physical-assumption",
          "thm-tdmath-conditional-contact-calibrator": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, Theorem 4.8 and §§IV.A–IV.C",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "André Thess, The Lieb–Yngvason Machine: A didactical tool for the definition of entropy in classical thermodynamics, §§2–4",
              "url": "https://www.dlr.de/de/tt/downloads/dokumente/institut/institutsleitung/Entropie.pdf/@@download/file"
            },
            {
              "title": "Goddard, The second law of thermodynamics as variation on a theme of Carathéodory, §§2–3",
              "url": "https://escholarship.org/content/qt27k6m59q/qt27k6m59q.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 13,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Conditional consequence of the adopted A1–A7, S1–S3, T1–T5 and C1 assumptions for the declared finite thermal-contact family; not an unconditional law for real materials."
      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-td-entropy-under-product-comparison",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Entropy representation for finite separated products",
        "statement": "Let $I$ be a nonempty, possibly infinite set of primitive system labels. Assume the declared family includes every finite separated product and every finite thermal join, and that every finite join-closed subfamily satisfies the hypotheses of [[pthm-td-contact-product-comparison]]. Also assume the explicit finite-factor representation of [[def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison]] and the Axiom of Choice. Then there is a single dimensionless function $\\widetilde S$ on all states of the declared finite-factor family that is additive under separation, extensive under every admitted positive scaling, and characterizes every equal-composition comparison:\n\n$$ A\\prec B\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\widetilde S(A)\\le \\widetilde S(B). $$\n\nFor each simple chart, A7 also makes $\\widetilde S$ concave on every convex region where the stated recombination operation is admitted. The result does not set the entropy unit to $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, determine additive constants across different reaction compositions, or establish empirical validity of the contact assumptions.",
        "deps": [
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison",
          "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6",
          "post-td-simple-system-geometric-axioms",
          "pthm-td-contact-product-comparison",
          "thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6": "physical-assumption",
          "post-td-simple-system-geometric-axioms": "physical-assumption",
          "pthm-td-contact-product-comparison": "physical-result",
          "thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, Theorems 2.2 and 4.8 and §IV.B",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "André Thess, The Lieb–Yngvason Machine: A didactical tool for the definition of entropy in classical thermodynamics, §§2–4",
              "url": "https://www.dlr.de/de/tt/downloads/dokumente/institut/institutsleitung/Entropie.pdf/@@download/file"
            },
            {
              "title": "Goddard, The second law of thermodynamics as variation on a theme of Carathéodory, §§2–3",
              "url": "https://escholarship.org/content/qt27k6m59q/qt27k6m59q.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 14,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "An order representation for the declared finite family of simple systems under the adopted contact assumptions; the entropy is dimensionless until an independent physical calibration fixes its unit."
      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-td-universal-mixing-entropy",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Conditional weak entropy principle across mixing channels",
        "statement": "Let $\\mathcal G\\ne\\varnothing$ be a finite-factor-closed network of [[def-td-cross-type-reaction-network]]. Assume the contact hypotheses of [[pthm-td-entropy-under-product-comparison]] provide a finite real baseline entropy $\\sigma_\\Gamma$ on each type. Also assume that network satisfies A1–A6, its exact decorated-chain cost $F$ has the no-sinks property defined there, and the Axiom of Choice. Then there is a real offset $B(\\Gamma)$ for each type, additive under finite composition and extensive under every positive scaling, such that the corrected entropy\n\n$$S_\\Gamma(x):=\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)+B(\\Gamma)$$\n\nis nondecreasing across every admitted channel transition: if $(\\Gamma,x)\\mathcal R(\\Gamma',y)$, then $S_\\Gamma(x)\\le S_{\\Gamma'}(y)$. The conclusion is weak monotonicity only. It does not assert a no-gap identity, uniqueness of $B$, strict increase, or a global entropy principle for networks that fail the stated hypotheses.",
        "deps": [
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "def-td-cross-type-reaction-network",
          "pthm-td-entropy-under-product-comparison",
          "thm-tdmath-splitting-cost-quotient",
          "thm-tdmath-unattained-cost-entropy-inference"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-td-cross-type-reaction-network": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "pthm-td-entropy-under-product-comparison": "physical-result",
          "thm-tdmath-splitting-cost-quotient": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-unattained-cost-entropy-inference": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, Theorem 6.1 and §VI.B",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 19,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Conditional entropy monotonicity across the declared cross-type mixing and reaction network; no-gap, uniqueness, strict increase, and empirical universality are not asserted."
      },
      {
        "id": "rem-td-entropy-construction-hypotheses",
        "kind": "remark",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Scope of the accessibility entropy construction",
        "statement": "The order relation in [[def-td-adiabatic-accessibility]] is a model-defined relation between equilibrium endpoints; it is not a measured trajectory law. A1–A6, A7, S1–S3, T1–T5 and C1 are adopted physical assumptions. The contact-product conclusion is conditional on the full list. In the inspected Lieb–Yngvason source, Theorem 4.8 is stated under T1–T4; the local supplier’s proof branch explicitly also needs the universal range T5 and the separate join-coherence bridge C1. The bare printed source interface does not discharge those stronger inputs.\n\nThe product representation $\\widetilde S$ is a dimensionless order parameter normalized by a chosen strict meter. A physical entropy reported in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$ requires an external scale calibration and stated uncertainty. The additive constants for composition types are not fixed by the no-mixing theorem; the mixing theorem fixes only a weak monotonicity selection under its extra hypotheses.\n\nThe arbitrary-family calibration supplier assumes an explicit finite-factor representation and the Axiom of Choice for simultaneous reference/calibrator selections. The mixing supplier further requires an actual cross-type transition network, finite decorated-chain costs, A1–A6 on those transitions, and the no-sinks condition. It does not establish that arbitrary mixing or reaction channels satisfy these conditions. No-gap equality, uniqueness of reaction offsets, or strict entropy increase requires additional hypotheses and is not asserted here.\n\nThe Carathéodory treatment of Goddard is a distinct route based on local inaccessibility. It supports the conceptual distinction between accessibility assumptions and derived consequences, but it is not a second proof of the convex-contact product theorem.",
        "deps": [
          "post-td-equilibrium-join-coherence",
          "post-td-universal-thermal-range",
          "pthm-td-contact-product-comparison",
          "pthm-td-entropy-under-product-comparison",
          "pthm-td-universal-mixing-entropy"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "post-td-equilibrium-join-coherence": "physical-assumption",
          "post-td-universal-thermal-range": "physical-assumption",
          "pthm-td-contact-product-comparison": "physical-result",
          "pthm-td-entropy-under-product-comparison": "physical-result",
          "pthm-td-universal-mixing-entropy": "physical-result"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §§II.D–II.F, IV.B–IV.C and VI",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "André Thess, The Lieb–Yngvason Machine: A didactical tool for the definition of entropy in classical thermodynamics, §§2–4",
              "url": "https://www.dlr.de/de/tt/downloads/dokumente/institut/institutsleitung/Entropie.pdf/@@download/file"
